            <ArticleTitle>Shoulder joint impairment among Finns aged 30 years or over: prevalence, risk factors and co-morbidity.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>656-62</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To describe the prevalence, risk factors and consequences of shoulder joint impairment in the population.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A representative sample (n = 7217) of the Finnish population aged &gt; or = 30 yr participated in a health examination survey (the Mini-Finland Health Survey). The design of the survey allowed an independent assessment of disability, reported shoulder pain, shoulder joint impairment and major chronic co-morbidity.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Shoulder impairment was observed in 8.8%, while pain was reported by 30%. The prevalence of shoulder pain decreased among the elderly, whereas impairments increased up to 20% of those aged 75-80 yr. In addition to age, sex, previous injury to the shoulder joint and a history of physically heavy work, diabetes was associated with shoulder impairment (OR 1.6, 95% CI 1.2-2.1). Shoulder impairment was associated with disability (adjusted OR 2.0, CI 1.6-2.5).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Shoulder impairment is an important component of ill health among the elderly, and cannot be reduced to reported pain alone.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
